On Jul 27, 6:03=A0am, faibistes <faibis...@gmail.com> wrote:

> So, in my opinion, nethack (without conducts) is a solved game, and PR
> is a winning strategy.


Every careful strategy is a winning one, until you make the first
mistake.  Often, YASD's can be easily traced back to that first player
error.

PR brings a few additional risks, such as range and wand attacks that
you somehow manage to avoid.

I'm not sure why this is a topic for debate though.  It's a "personal
habits" thing, and if there's space for controversy, I'm not seeing
it.

I tend to go for the first round of Protection at the start of all my
games.  But I'm not playing "PR" per se, it's more like I'm taking
baby steps to learn what pacifist conduct might be like.  I tend to do
pacifist conduct right up until I'm bored with it (usually once I get
a boon, and early protection + shop loot counts), and I decide that
I'd rather that character live than die...  It's also about the time
that I get bored with the careful play, or I'll get mad at some
monster in particular (nymphs are common) and I'll just go berserk at
that point to kill it.  So it's frequently pacifist/protectioneer zero
to seven in a matter of seconds.

Still, why is this an issue?  It's a "what side of your bread do you
butter" or "do you hang the toilet paper over the front of the roll or
down the back" kind of thing.  *shrug*.



